## The Audit Loop

The phases are **one iteration**, not a one-shot script. Fix what the audit finds, then re-run the whole thing from the capture step. Repeat until one complete iteration finds nothing and changes nothing.

```text
setup:  Phase 0, Phase 1            # once — scope clean, scratch route up, dev server running

iteration = 1
loop:
  Phase 2   re-capture the full matrix
  Phase 3   all 71 checks
  Phase 4   fidelity

  if zero ❌ and zero fixes applied this pass:
    converged → Phase 5

  if a check is ❌ that an earlier iteration already fixed:   stop → 🔴 oscillating
  if the same check is ❌ twice in a row after a fix:          stop → 🔴 stuck
  if iteration == 5:                                          stop → 🔴 budget exhausted

  apply a fix for every ❌
  re-run the Phase 0 scoped type-check + lint
  iteration += 1
  go to loop                        # from Phase 2, never from mid-checklist
```

**Convergence requires both halves: no failures *and* no edits.** A pass that finds nothing but where you still adjusted something has not converged — the adjustment is unverified until a capture proves it. This is also why **you may not edit anything that is not a recorded ❌**. Gold-plating a spacing value you merely dislike restarts the loop for no logged reason and is the one reliable way to make it never terminate.

### Why the full matrix, every iteration

Resuming mid-checklist after a fix is the mistake. Rendered fixes cascade harder than static ones, because CSS is global and a class that repairs one width routinely damages another:

- `overflow-hidden` to stop the 320 overflow (**2.1**) clips a focus ring or a dropdown at 1440 (**2.4**)
- `min-w-0` per **3.3** lets the title finally shrink — and now it needs `truncate` (**2.2**)
- `<h1>` → `<h2>` per **5.1** leaves an `<h3>` beneath it, so the outline now skips a level (**5.2**)
- `aria-hidden` on an icon per **6.4** strips the button's only accessible name (**6.3**)
- `bg-card` to fix dark mode (**2.3**) drops the card below 4.5:1 against the section background in *light* (**2.5**)
- `max-w-*` to bound the 2560 case (**3.9**) narrows the block at 1440 and breaks fidelity (Phase 4)

Each of those lands in a section that already passed. A capture only proves what was true when it was taken.

### The one exemption

A fix that **cannot** alter rendering lets you reuse the existing captures for §1–§4 and re-verify only §5–§6. That covers exactly: adding or editing `aria-*`, `alt`, `datetime`, `id`, `scope`, or `role`; swapping a heading tag while its classes stay identical (Tailwind preflight leaves headings unstyled, so `<h2 className="text-lg">` → `<h3 className="text-lg">` is visually identical); and wrapping content in `<figure>`, `<article>` or `<time>` with no added classes.

Anything touching `className`, element nesting, or a component boundary is a full re-capture. If you are unsure which side a fix falls on, it is a full re-capture — the exemption exists to stop an agent from quietly skipping captures under cost pressure, not to create a fast path.

### The edit surface — what a fix may touch

**Every fix lands in the block's own file**, plus files inside its own `components/` subfolder if it is multi-file. That is the entire writable surface. For the duration of the audit everything else is **read-only**:

| Off limits | Why |
| :--------- | :-- |
| `src/components/ui/**` | Shadcn primitives are shared by every block in the library |
| `src/app/globals.css` | The tokens are the product's contract with the buyer (**7.1**) |
| `tailwind.config.*`, `tsconfig.json`, `eslint.config.mjs`, `package.json` | A block that needs a config change is not zero-config (`code-review` **13.8**) |
| Any other block, shared helper, or app file | Not this block's concern |

The sole exception is the Phase 1 scratch route, which this skill creates and deletes itself.

The reason is blast radius: retuning a token in `globals.css` to fix one block's contrast silently restyles **every other block in the library**, and you will not notice until someone else's audit fails. One block's defect is never worth a library-wide change made without looking at the library.

**When a fix genuinely requires an out-of-scope change, stop. Do not work around it.**

The workaround is always available and always wrong — hardcode the value in the block and the contrast passes. You have then traded a scope violation for a `code-review` §2.4 / §11.6 violation, and shipped a block that breaks the moment the buyer changes their theme (**7.1**). That is strictly worse than the original defect, and it hides instead of reporting.

Take the 🔴 **stuck** exit instead, and name three things: the check that failed, the file that would need to change, and what change it needs. That is a decision for whoever owns the design system, and handing it over intact is the correct outcome — not a failure of the audit.

### Stopping

Four exits, and only the first is a pass:

| Exit | Condition | What it means |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 🟢 **Converged** | one full iteration, zero ❌, zero edits | ship it |
| 🔴 **Oscillating** | a check fails that an earlier fix resolved | two checks are in genuine tension — a design decision, not another fix attempt. Report both and the widths involved |
| 🔴 **Stuck** | same check ❌ twice after being "fixed", **or the only real fix is outside the edit surface** | the cause is outside the block, or the fix is not addressing it. Name the file that would have to change |
| 🔴 **Budget** | 5 iterations without converging | stop and report; do not keep going |

The 320-versus-1440 pair above is the canonical oscillation: clamping overflow at the small width clips at the large one, and un-clipping restores the overflow. Do not attempt a third swing at it — say which two checks conflict and hand the tradeoff back.

By iteration 3, if new defects are still appearing in sections that previously passed, the fixes are too broad. Narrow them before spending iteration 4.

### Cost

A full matrix is **15 captures** (320 light, plus 7 widths × 2 themes). At five iterations that is 75. This is the expensive part of the audit, which is the reason the loop is written down instead of left to judgment: an undocumented loop gets silently truncated. Budget for 2–3 iterations on a normal block.

The Phase 0 scoped type-check and lint re-run every iteration — fast enough, and scoped so an unrelated error elsewhere in the app never stalls the loop. No production build runs at any point; see Phase 0 for what that leaves uncovered.

