## Phase 0 — Preconditions

**Scope is whatever the caller named** — a single block file, or a folder when the block is multi-file. Only errors inside that scope block this audit. A pre-existing type error elsewhere in the app is not this block's problem and must not stall it.

```bash
TARGET=src/components/uiable/blocks/<category>/<block-name>    # file or folder, as given

npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json | grep -F "$TARGET"          # scoped types
npx eslint "$TARGET"                                            # scoped lint
```

Stop here if either reports anything inside `$TARGET`; a type error means what renders is not what you reviewed.

> **Do not "optimize" this to `npx tsc --noEmit "$TARGET"`.** Passing files to `tsc` explicitly makes it **ignore `tsconfig.json` altogether** — so `paths` goes with it, every `@/components/ui/*` import fails to resolve, and you get a screen of fabricated errors about the block's own dependencies. The `-p` flag is what keeps the aliases, `jsx` and `strict` settings alive; the `grep` is what narrows the *reporting*. Checking is project-wide either way, and that is fine — it costs one command and cannot lie to you.
>
> On a large monorepo where the full pass is genuinely too slow, narrow the *checking* with a throwaway config that inherits the real one, rather than by dropping it:
>
> ```jsonc
> // tsconfig.audit.json — temporary, delete with the scratch route
> { "extends": "./tsconfig.json", "include": ["src/components/uiable/blocks/<category>/<block-name>/**/*"] }
> ```
>
> TypeScript still pulls in everything the block imports, which is exactly what you want checked, and skips the rest of the app.

No production build runs in this audit — it is a project-level concern, not a per-block one, and a full Next build per iteration is not worth its cost here. Be aware of what that leaves uncovered: **server/client boundary violations, non-serializable props crossing it, and a missing `"use client"` surface at build and not under `npm run dev`.** Catch those in CI or a pre-merge check; this gate will not see them.

