## Phase 2 — Capture matrix

Capture at each width below in **both themes**.

| Width       | Represents                   | Why this one                                              |
| :---------- | :--------------------------- | :-------------------------------------------------------- |
| `320`       | smallest real phone           | overflow and clipping surface here first — light only       |
| `390`       | baseline mobile               | stacking, tap targets, truncation                           |
| `767`       | **just below `md:`**          | the widest mobile layout — where mobile breaks, not at 768  |
| `768`       | `md:` — tablet                | the first layout switch                                     |
| `1023`      | **just below `lg:`**          | the widest tablet layout                                    |
| `1440`      | design width                  | the frame the design was drawn at                           |
| `2560`      | ultrawide                     | is content still bounded, or stretched                      |
| `844 × 390` | phone landscape               | short viewport — breaks full-height centered heroes         |

**Test just below each breakpoint, not only at it.** A layout switches cleanly at exactly 768 and fails at 767, because the last few pixels of the previous range are where content is most cramped. Auditing only at breakpoint values is the most common way a responsive pass misses real bugs.

Theme switching depends on the app's strategy — with `next-themes` on the `class` strategy, toggle `document.documentElement.classList` between `dark` and nothing; otherwise emulate `prefers-color-scheme`. **Confirm the toggle took effect in the screenshot before trusting a clean dark pass** — a light screenshot labeled "dark" is the most common false green in this gate.

