## Phase 3 — Audit checklist

71 checks in seven groups. Mark each ✅ PASS / ❌ FAIL / ⏭️ N/A.

> ## ⚠️ §4.9 is a hard blocker
>
> **Sibling spacing must come from `gap`.** This is CRITICAL in `code-review` §2.6, and that severity carries here — **4.9** is the same rule, proven against the rendered DOM instead of the class strings.
>
> A single violation means the block is **not ready**, no matter how clean the other 70 checks are. It cannot be marked ⏭️ N/A. It cannot be deferred as a follow-up. It cannot be traded against a passing verdict anywhere else, and **the loop does not converge while it is ❌.**
>
> It is the most-violated rule in the entire audit because it fails silently: margin-based spacing *looks* correct in every capture. The only thing that catches it is running the assertion — so run it, every iteration, at both widths. A block that was never asserted has not passed **4.9**; it has an unverified **4.9**.

### How to work it: one capture at a time

Do **not** walk the checklist top to bottom — that makes you reopen every screenshot six times. Take one capture, evaluate everything that width can reveal, record results, move to the next. Order: **320 → 390 → 767 → 768 → 1023 → 1440 → 2560 → landscape.**

The routing tables below are **pointers into the checklist, not a restatement of it.** The numbered checks in §1–§6 are the source of truth; the cues here are only there to tell you what to look for while a given capture is on screen. If the two ever disagree, the check wins.

**On every capture, both themes:**

| Check | Cue |
| :---- | :-- |
| **2.2** | text clipping or overlap |
| **2.3** | dark legibility — on each dark capture |
| **2.5** | contrast, sampled from rendered pixels |
| **2.7** | image aspect ratio |
| **4.4** | centering gaps measured left/right, top/bottom |
| **4.8** | shared edges actually align |

**Per viewport — what only this width reveals:**

| Capture | Checks | What this width is for |
| :------ | :----- | :--------------------- |
| **320** light | **2.1**, **3.3**, **3.4**, **3.11** | The overflow gate. Assert `scrollWidth <= innerWidth` before anything else — if it fails here, **3.3** (`min-w-0`) and **3.4** (unbroken strings) are the two causes to check, in that order |
| **390** | **3.5**, **3.11**, **4.6**, **3.13** | Baseline mobile. Re-capture with ~3× the text for **3.5**; wrapped text is what exposes **4.6** icon alignment |
| **767** | **3.2**, **3.3**, **3.6**, **3.1** | Widest mobile — the cramped end of the range. Second most likely place **3.3** bites. Confirm via **3.1** that this is still the mobile layout, not `md:` |
| **768** | **3.1**, **3.6**, **4.5**, **3.14** | First layout switch. Did the grid actually change columns, did `gap` scale, did centering survive the switch |
| **1023** | **3.2**, **3.6**, **3.1** | Widest tablet. Same cramped-end logic as 767, one breakpoint up |
| **1440** | **3.10**, **2.4**, **3.15**, **4.7** | Design width — Phase 4 fidelity is compared here. Line length, focus rings, image `sizes`, and optical centering of icons in round buttons (largest capture, so the easiest place to see a 1–2px offset) |
| **2560** | **3.9**, **3.10**, **4.1** | Ultrawide. Is content still bounded by `max-w-*`, or is prose stretched past reading width |
| **844×390** | **3.8**, **3.13** | Landscape. Short viewport — `min-h-screen` heroes clip, sticky elements eat the fold |

**Not visible in any capture.** These need browser state, not a screenshot — run them once, separately, and never mark them passed off a screenshot that merely looked fine:

| Check | How |
| :---- | :-- |
| **3.7** | emulate 200% zoom |
| **3.12** | inspect the viewport meta for `user-scalable=no` / `maximum-scale` |
| **2.6** | emulate `prefers-reduced-motion` |
| **2.8** | select the text and try to copy it |
| **3.16** | read the classes for `pl-*`/`pr-*`/`ml-*`/`mr-*` on directional spacing |
| **4.2**, **4.3** | read the classes — a capture that *looks* centered does not tell you whether the technique is sound. `my-auto` and hand-tuned padding center correctly right up until the content length changes |
| **4.9** | run the gap assertion in the console at **390 and 1440** — responsive gaps (`gap-4 md:gap-8`) resolve differently at each, so one width does not prove the other |
| **7.1** | override the tokens to hostile values and re-capture 390 + 1440 in both themes — this is a second mini-matrix, not a glance |
| **7.2** | read computed `animation-name` and `font-family` off the rendered elements |
| **7.3** | click every visible control and confirm it does something, or is not presented as a control |
| **7.4** | read the source — where does the copy actually live |
| **7.5** | scale the 1440 capture to ~400px wide and look at it |
| **7.6** | run the hand-rolled-component sweep in the console — the DOM is where the missing behaviour shows |
| **7.7** | run the dead-CSS script at **390 and 1440** — a class inert at one width may be live at the other, so only remove what is dead at both |
| **§1**, **§5**, **§6** | console, DOM and keyboard — run once on the 1440 capture's live page, not per width |

§5 and §6 are width-independent: the heading outline and ARIA wiring do not change with the viewport, so audit them once rather than eight times. The exception is **6.11** tab order — re-check it at 390 if the layout reorders anything at mobile, because visual order changed and DOM order did not.

### §1 Render correctness (5)

- [ ] **1.1** **Zero console errors.** Read the console, not just the page — key warnings, failed images, and thrown effects all log without visibly breaking layout
- [ ] **1.2** **No hydration mismatch.** `Text content did not match` / `Hydration failed`. Causes: `Date.now()`, `new Date()`, `Math.random()`, `toLocaleString()`, `window`/`document` read during render. Invisible to any static pass and the main bug class that reaches production from a clean review
- [ ] **1.3** **Every image loads.** Check the network panel for 404s — a bad `next/image` src renders as empty space, which reads as a spacing bug
- [ ] **1.4** **No layout shift on load.** Images without dimensions and webfont swaps both push content down after first paint
- [ ] **1.5** **Interactive elements respond.** Click, hover and tab through everything. A `Dialog` that never opens passes every static check. **Clicking a link must not navigate away** — blocks ship `href="#"` placeholders (`code-review` §13.7), so a jump to the top of the page is expected and a navigation is a surviving real destination. Treat leaving the page as a §13.7 finding, and note that it also invalidates the captures taken after it

### §2 Visual defects (8)

- [ ] **2.1** **No horizontal overflow at 320.** Assert it — `document.documentElement.scrollWidth <= window.innerWidth` — do not eyeball. A few px of overflow is invisible and real
- [ ] **2.2** **No text clipping or overlap** at any width, either theme
- [ ] **2.3** **Dark mode is genuinely legible.** Hunt un-themed white cards, text near background luminance, vanishing borders. Compare themes side by side rather than judging dark alone
- [ ] **2.4** **Focus rings visible in both themes.** A ring that reads on white often disappears on a dark surface
- [ ] **2.5** **Body text clears 4.5:1**, large text 3:1, in **both** themes. Sample the rendered pixels; do not infer from token names
- [ ] **2.6** **`prefers-reduced-motion` is respected.** Emulate it — animations should shorten or stop, not merely slow down
- [ ] **2.7** **Images hold aspect ratio** at every width — no stretch, no unintended crop
- [ ] **2.8** **Text stays selectable.** No `select-none` on content that a reader would copy

### §3 Responsive behavior (16)

- [ ] **3.1** **Layout actually switches.** `md:grid-cols-3` in a class string is not evidence of three columns at 768 — confirm in the capture
- [ ] **3.2** **Just-below-breakpoint widths hold** (767, 1023) — see the Phase 2 note
- [ ] **3.3** **Flex and grid children carry `min-w-0` where they must shrink.** Children default to `min-width: auto` and refuse to shrink below content size — the single most common cause of unexplained overflow
- [ ] **3.4** **Long unbroken strings wrap.** Drop in a 60-character URL or email; needs `break-words` / `overflow-wrap`. Untested, it overflows every mobile width
- [ ] **3.5** **Long content does not break the layout.** Replace one title and one description with ~3× the text, re-capture at 390. Blocks are built with tidy placeholder copy and fail on real content
- [ ] **3.6** **Spacing rhythm holds across breakpoints.** Right at 1440 and cramped at 768 means `gap` needs breakpoints (`code-review` §7.6)
- [ ] **3.7** **200% zoom does not break it** (WCAG 1.4.4). Content reflows, nothing is clipped or overlapped
- [ ] **3.8** **Phone landscape works.** At 844×390, a `min-h-screen` hero with vertically centered content typically clips top and bottom
- [ ] **3.9** **Content stays bounded at 2560.** A block with no `max-w-*` on its wrapper stretches text to unreadable line lengths
- [ ] **3.10** **Line length stays readable** — roughly 65–75 characters at desktop. Full-width prose is a design defect even when nothing is technically broken
- [ ] **3.11** **Touch targets are ≥ 44×44px at mobile**, including icon-only buttons and close affordances
- [ ] **3.12** **Zoom is not blocked.** No `user-scalable=no` or `maximum-scale=1` reaching the viewport meta
- [ ] **3.13** **Sticky and fixed elements do not cover content** at short viewport heights
- [ ] **3.14** **Horizontal scroll containers show an affordance** — a partial next card, a fade, or arrows. A carousel that looks complete hides its remaining items
- [ ] **3.15** **`next/image` with `fill` has a correct `sizes`.** Wrong `sizes` silently ships a desktop-weight image to phones
- [ ] **3.16** **Logical spacing properties for RTL safety** — `ps-*`/`pe-*`/`ms-*`/`me-*` over `pl-*`/`pr-*`/`ml-*`/`mr-*` on anything directional

### §4 Layout, centering & alignment (9)

- [ ] **4.1** **Horizontal centering is `mx-auto` on a width-bounded element**, or `justify-center` on a flex parent — never `text-center` standing in for block layout
- [ ] **4.2** **Vertical centering is `items-center` on a flex/grid parent with a real height context.** `my-auto` and hand-tuned padding both drift the moment content length changes
- [ ] **4.3** **Both-axis centering is one declaration** — `grid place-items-center`, or `flex items-center justify-center`. Not `top-1/2 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2` guesswork
- [ ] **4.4** **Centering is measured, not eyeballed.** Compare left vs. right (and top vs. bottom) gaps in the capture. An unequal pair means a stray margin, an asymmetric padding, or a scrollbar-width shift
- [ ] **4.5** **Centering survives every breakpoint.** A `md:text-left` that never resets its container's `justify-center`, or vice versa, is the usual culprit
- [ ] **4.6** **Icon-beside-text alignment is deliberate** — `items-center` for single-line pairs, `items-baseline` or `items-start` when the text can wrap to two lines. `items-center` on wrapped text floats the icon to the vertical middle of the block
- [ ] **4.7** **Icons are optically centered in circular or square buttons.** SVG viewBox padding routinely makes a geometrically centered glyph look off by a pixel or two
- [ ] **4.8** **Shared edges actually align.** Card tops, column starts, and baseline grids line up across siblings — not off by a few px from inconsistent padding
- [ ] **4.9** **All sibling spacing comes from `gap`, proven in the DOM (⚠️ CRITICAL — hard blocker).** `code-review` §2.6 owns this as a CRITICAL authoring rule against the class strings; this is the rendered proof, and it catches cases a grep cannot. The rule applies at **every** nesting level and at every child count — a two, three or four column card grid, a stacked pair of paragraphs, and an icon sitting beside its own label are all the same case. The space between siblings is `gap-*` on their parent, never `m-*`/`mt-*`/`ms-*` on the children.

  Assert it instead of reading classes:

  ```js
  const noGap = (v) => v === "normal" || parseFloat(v) === 0

  ;[...document.querySelectorAll("section, section *")]
    .filter((el) => el.children.length >= 2)
    .flatMap((el) => {
      const s = getComputedStyle(el)
      const marginKids = [...el.children].filter((k) => {
        const m = getComputedStyle(k)
        return ["marginTop", "marginRight", "marginBottom", "marginLeft"].some(
          (p) => parseFloat(m[p]) > 0,
        )
      })
      const out = []
      if (marginKids.length)
        out.push({ el, issue: `${marginKids.length} child(ren) carry margin`, marginKids })
      if (/flex|grid/.test(s.display) && noGap(s.rowGap) && noGap(s.columnGap))
        out.push({ el, issue: "flex/grid parent with 2+ children and no gap" })
      return out
    })
  ```

  Every row it returns is a violation — a child carrying margin, or a multi-child flex/grid parent with no gap at all. Fix it in the source and route the fix through `code-review` §2.6, since that check is CRITICAL there.

  **Do not mistake a parent's own padding for sibling spacing.** `px-4` on a button that wraps an icon and a label is correct internal padding and not a finding. `mr-2` on the icon *inside* that button is the violation. The test is whether the value sits **between two siblings** or **around their parent**.

  The only legitimate exception is spacing that must skip one specific child, which `gap` cannot express. Record that as an accepted deviation in the summary rather than leaving it to read as an oversight.

### §5 Semantic HTML & SEO structure (14)

- [ ] **5.1** **The block does not claim `<h1>`.** A page has exactly one, and the page owns it — blocks almost always start at `<h2>`. A block hardcoding `<h1>` breaks every page that composes two of them
- [ ] **5.2** **Heading levels do not skip.** `<h2>` → `<h4>` is a broken outline even when it looks right
- [ ] **5.3** **Heading level is chosen by structure, not size.** Reach for `<h2 className="text-lg">`, never `<h4>` because the size happened to match
- [ ] **5.4** **The rendered outline is a valid tree.** Extract it — `[...document.querySelectorAll("h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6")].map(h => h.tagName + " " + h.textContent)` — and read it as a table of contents. If it does not make sense as one, neither will it to a crawler
- [ ] **5.5** **`<section>` has an accessible name.** An unnamed `<section>` is a `<div>` to assistive tech — wire `aria-labelledby` to its heading's `id`
- [ ] **5.6** **Lists are `<ul>`/`<ol>`/`<li>`** — not `<div>`s with bullet glyphs. Feature grids and nav menus are lists
- [ ] **5.7** **`<nav>` carries an `aria-label`** when a page has more than one
- [ ] **5.8** **`<article>` for self-contained, syndicatable content** — blog cards, testimonials, press items
- [ ] **5.9** **`<button>` for actions, `<a href>` for navigation.** An `<a>` without `href` is not focusable and not announced as a link
- [ ] **5.10** **Link text is descriptive out of context.** "Read more" ×6 in one block gives a screen-reader user six identical links
- [ ] **5.11** **`alt` matches the image's role.** Descriptive for content images; **`alt=""` for decorative ones** — empty is correct and required there, and it is what `code-review` §8.2 gets wrong
- [ ] **5.12** **`<time datetime="...">` for dates**, so the machine-readable value matches the display string
- [ ] **5.13** **`<figure>`/`<figcaption>`, `<address>`, `<blockquote>` where the content is literally that.** A quote in a `<div>` loses its meaning
- [ ] **5.14** **Structured data where the block type warrants it** — FAQ, Product, Breadcrumb, Review. This is the concrete SEO win in a block audit, and the most commonly skipped one

### §6 ARIA & assistive tech (12)

- [ ] **6.1** **No ARIA where native HTML already works.** First rule of ARIA. `<button>` beats `<div role="button" tabIndex={0}>` every time
- [ ] **6.2** **No redundant roles** — `<nav role="navigation">`, `<ul role="list">` add nothing
- [ ] **6.3** **Every icon-only control has an accessible name.** Verify by what the browser announces, not by the attribute's presence
- [ ] **6.4** **Decorative icons are `aria-hidden="true"`** — and confirm the icon is not the element's only label before hiding it
- [ ] **6.5** **`aria-labelledby` / `aria-describedby` point at IDs that exist.** Check in the DOM; a typo'd reference fails silently with no console error
- [ ] **6.6** **IDs are unique on the page.** A block rendered twice with hardcoded IDs breaks every `aria-labelledby` and `<label for>` in both copies — a real bug in registry blocks, which are built to be reused
- [ ] **6.7** **`aria-expanded` on disclosure triggers, and it tracks state.** Toggle it and re-read the attribute
- [ ] **6.8** **`aria-current="page"` on the active nav item**
- [ ] **6.9** **Async status is announced** via `aria-live` — a form result that only appears visually is silent to a screen reader
- [ ] **6.10** **Form controls have real `<label>`s.** A placeholder is not a label; it disappears on input and is inconsistently announced
- [ ] **6.11** **Tab order matches visual order.** Tab through the whole block; no positive `tabIndex`, nothing focusable that is visually hidden, nothing skipped
- [ ] **6.12** **Shadcn a11y behavior survived customization.** Dialogs and dropdowns ship focus trapping, focus return and escape handling — verify a customized one still traps, still returns focus to its trigger, and still closes on Escape

### §7 Product readiness (7)

These blocks are **sold**. The code is the deliverable, and the preview is the sales pitch — so a block that renders perfectly in our app and fails in a buyer's project is a defective product, not a styling nit. This group exists because none of the other six ask whether the block survives leaving our repo.

- [ ] **7.1** **It survives the buyer's theme, not just ours.** Every token in §11.1 is a variable the buyer redefines. Override them to hostile values and re-check: set `--primary` to a pale yellow (`#fde047`) and confirm `text-primary-foreground` still reads on it; set `--radius` to `0` and then `1.5rem` and confirm nothing overlaps or clips. A block whose contrast only works because our primary happens to be mid-blue is broken for every buyer who changes it — and changing it is the first thing they do
- [ ] **7.2** **Zero-config install, proven in the DOM.** `code-review` §13.8 greps this file; here, read computed styles for what the block actually depends on. Check `animation-name` on animated elements resolves to a keyframe Tailwind ships, and `font-family` resolves to the buyer's stack rather than a family only we load. This catches a dependency arriving through a Shadcn primitive, which the grep never sees
- [ ] **7.3** **No dead affordances.** These blocks are static by design, and that is fine — but a control that *looks* interactive and does nothing reads as broken, not as a placeholder. Every visible affordance either works self-contained (tabs switch, accordions open, carousels move) or is not shown as a control at all. Links are the settled exception: `href="#"` per `code-review` §13.7 is the agreed convention. A Submit button that silently does nothing is not
- [ ] **7.4** **Content is swappable from one place.** The buyer's first action is replacing every string. Confirm the copy lives in one module-scope array or object (`code-review` §1.4, §9.2) rather than scattered through the JSX — the perf rationale in §9.2 is secondary here; the real reason is that a buyer who has to hunt through 200 lines of markup to change a heading will say so in a review
- [ ] **7.5** **It reads as a gallery thumbnail.** The block is sold from a preview image, so the thumbnail *is* the pitch. Scale the 1440 capture down to ~400px wide and look at it: the hierarchy should still be legible, the focal point obvious, and the block recognisable as what it is. A layout that only resolves at full size will not sell
- [ ] **7.6** **Nothing hand-rolled that a Shadcn component already provides.** `code-review` §3.10 holds the full raw-element → component table and finds these by reading; this finds them by their **missing behaviour**, which is the half a source read cannot see. A hand-rolled dropdown looks fine in a screenshot and in the JSX — what it lacks is focus trapping, Escape handling, arrow-key navigation, `aria-expanded` bookkeeping and focus return, all of which the real component ships. Buyers are paying for a Shadcn-based library; bespoke divs wearing ARIA are the thing they are paying not to write.

  ```js
  const NATIVE = "button, a[href], input, select, textarea, summary, [role='button'], [tabindex]"
  const CONTROL = "button, input, [role='button'], [role='tab'], [role='checkbox'], [role='switch']"
  const rolled = []

  for (const el of document.querySelectorAll("section, section *")) {
    const tag = el.tagName.toLowerCase()
    const role = el.getAttribute("role")

    if (role && /^(button|tab|tablist|menu|menuitem|checkbox|switch|radio|dialog|tooltip|progressbar|separator)$/.test(role)
        && !["button", "input", "dialog", "progress", "hr"].includes(tag))
      rolled.push({ el, signal: `role="${role}" on <${tag}>` })

    if (el.classList.contains("cursor-pointer") && !el.matches(NATIVE))
      rolled.push({ el, signal: "cursor-pointer on a non-interactive element" })

    for (const a of ["aria-expanded", "aria-selected", "aria-checked", "aria-pressed"])
      if (el.hasAttribute(a) && !el.matches(CONTROL))
        rolled.push({ el, signal: `${a} on <${tag}> with no control role` })
  }
  console.table(rolled)
  ```

  Each row is a signal, not an automatic failure — read it against the block and decide:

  | Signal in the DOM | Reach for |
  | :---------------- | :-------- |
  | `role="button"`, or `cursor-pointer` on a `div` | `Button` — or it is a dead affordance (**7.3**) |
  | `role="tab"` / `role="tablist"` | `Tabs` |
  | `role="dialog"` with no focus trap | `Dialog` · `AlertDialog` · `Sheet` · `Drawer` |
  | `role="menu"` / `role="menuitem"` | `DropdownMenu` · `ContextMenu` |
  | `aria-expanded` on a `div` | `Accordion` · `Collapsible` · `DropdownMenu` |
  | `role="checkbox"` / `role="switch"` | `Checkbox` · `Switch` |
  | `role="progressbar"` | `Progress` |
  | `role="separator"`, or a bare 1px border `div` | `Separator` |
  | `role="tooltip"` | `Tooltip` · `HoverCard` |
  | `overflow-auto` with hand-written scrollbar CSS | `ScrollArea` |
  | a cluster of pulsing grey placeholder `div`s | `Skeleton` |

  The `cursor-pointer` signal is the highest-yield one and fires constantly: it is either a control that should be a `Button`, or an affordance that does nothing and belongs to **7.3**. Both are findings, so nothing it returns is safe to wave through. For any signal not in this table, go to `code-review` §3.10 for the component.

- [ ] **7.7** **No dead CSS, proven against computed styles.** `code-review` §2.8 catalogues 29 patterns of valid-but-inert Tailwind classes and finds them by reading; this proves the structural ones from the DOM, which is the only way to be certain. Buyers read this source — a block carrying twenty classes that do nothing looks careless regardless of how it renders, and that is a product defect, not a nit.

  ```js
  const dead = []
  for (const el of document.querySelectorAll("section, section *")) {
    const cl = [...el.classList]
    const s = getComputedStyle(el)
    const ps = el.parentElement && getComputedStyle(el.parentElement)
    const parentFlexGrid = ps && /flex|grid/.test(ps.display)
    const has = (re) => cl.filter((c) => re.test(c))

    // flex/grid child props with no flex/grid parent
    if (!parentFlexGrid)
      dead.push(...has(/^(items-|self-|justify-|order-|shrink|grow|basis-|flex-[0-9a-z])/).map((c) => [el, c, "parent is not flex/grid"]))
    // gap on a non-flex/grid element
    if (!/flex|grid/.test(s.display) && s.columnCount === "auto")
      dead.push(...has(/^gap(-|$)/).map((c) => [el, c, "element is not flex/grid"]))
    // offsets and z-index with no positioning
    if (s.position === "static") {
      dead.push(...has(/^(top-|right-|bottom-|left-|inset-)/).map((c) => [el, c, "element is static"]))
      if (!parentFlexGrid) dead.push(...has(/^-?z-/).map((c) => [el, c, "static and not a flex/grid item"]))
    }
    // border colour with zero border width
    if (["borderTopWidth","borderRightWidth","borderBottomWidth","borderLeftWidth"].every((p) => parseFloat(s[p]) === 0))
      dead.push(...has(/^border-(?!collapse|separate|spacing)[a-z]/).map((c) => [el, c, "border-width is 0"]))
    // h-full inside an auto-height parent
    if (cl.includes("h-full") && ps && ps.height === "auto")
      dead.push([el, "h-full", "parent height is auto"])
    // group-* / peer-* with no marker
    if (has(/^group-/).length && !el.closest(".group"))
      dead.push(...has(/^group-/).map((c) => [el, c, "no .group ancestor"]))
    if (has(/^peer-/).length && !el.parentElement?.querySelector(":scope > .peer"))
      dead.push(...has(/^peer-/).map((c) => [el, c, "no .peer sibling"]))
  }
  console.table(dead.map(([el, c, why]) => ({ class: c, why, el })))
  ```

  Remove every class it returns, then **list all of them in the report's Dead CSS section** with the reason. Two caveats before deleting: a class may be inert at *this* width but live at another (`md:items-center` on a parent that only becomes `flex` at `md:`), so run the script at **390 and 1440** and only remove what is dead at both; and a responsive or state variant is reported by its full name, so check whether the base or the variant is the dead half before cutting.


### Judgment — what the 71 checks do not name

**The checklist is the floor, not the ceiling.** It holds what recurs across many blocks; it cannot anticipate what one specific block gets wrong. After working the checks, look at the rendered block as a whole and ask: *what is wrong here that nothing above named?* The captures are already open — this costs one more look, and it is the only part of the audit that can catch something genuinely novel.

Fix what you find, under three rules:

1. **Fix it, do not merely note it** — held to the same standard as a numbered check, and re-captured on the next iteration. It counts as a ❌ for convergence: an iteration that fixed something has not converged.
2. **Record every one in the summary**, in its own section, with the same columns as the Change Log. A fix the report does not mention is indistinguishable from an unrequested edit (the no-gold-plating rule).
3. **Do not add it to this file.** One occurrence is an observation, not a rule.

Stay inside the edit surface, and keep the bar at *defect* — broken, unsafe, misleading, unusable — not preference. "I would have spaced this differently" is not a finding.

**When one earns a number:** it has appeared in **three separate blocks**, it is **mechanically checkable** (a DOM assertion or a rule applied without re-deriving the reasoning), and it applies to **blocks generally** rather than one category. Short of all three, it stays a judgment call. Seventy-one checks is already a lot to carry through eight captures; a check that fires once in fifty blocks costs more attention than it saves. The "Off-checklist fixes" section of past reports is the intake queue — a third appearance is the signal to write the rule.

